                <h2>Why Ann Arbor Homes Need Standby Generators</h2>
                <p>Michigan weather causes frequent power outages. Ice storms in winter, severe thunderstorms in summer, and aging infrastructure mean Washtenaw County residents experience an average of 3-5 outages per year. For homes with sump pumps, medical equipment, home offices, or well water, even a short outage can be catastrophic.</p>

                <p>A standby generator eliminates these concerns. The system monitors your power 24/7 and starts automatically within 10 seconds of an outage. No manual intervention required - it works whether you're home, at work, or on vacation.</p>

                <h2>Generac Guardian Series - Our Primary Installation</h2>
                <p>Bremray is a Generac Aligned Partner. We install Generac Guardian standby generators.</p>

                <div class="surface-panel">
                    <h3>22kW Guardian</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Whole-home coverage depends on your electrical loads</li>
                        <li>Central AC, heat, and appliances must be included in the load assessment</li>
                        <li>Aluminum enclosure with RhinoCoat finish</li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.generac.com/globalassets/products/residential/standby-generators/warranty/5-year-limited-warranty-air-cooled-standby-generator-warranty.pdf">5-year limited warranty</a> on applicable models; check the selected model's terms</li>
                        <li>WiFi monitoring via Mobile Link app</li>
                        <li>Quiet operation - 67 dB at 23 feet</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Complete installation includes:</strong> transfer switch, permits, gas connection. Contact for walkthrough.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="surface-panel">
                    <h3>24kW & 26kW Options</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Options for higher electrical loads, subject to a load assessment</li>
                        <li>Multiple AC units or heavy electrical loads</li>
                        <li>Pool equipment and hot tubs</li>
                        <li>Home-based businesses</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Complete professional installation.</strong> Contact for walkthrough and options.</p>
                </div>

                <p>Generator size depends on what you need to run, including starting loads. The site and available fuel supply matter too. See <a href="https://www.generac.com/resources/home-management/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">Generac's sizing guide</a>.</p>

                <h2>Natural Gas vs Propane Operation</h2>

                <h3>Natural Gas (Recommended When Available)</h3>
                <p>If you have natural gas service, this is the ideal fuel source:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Unlimited runtime during extended outages</li>
                    <li>No refueling needed</li>
                    <li>No tanks to rent or maintain</li>
                    <li>Lower operating cost per hour</li>
                    <li>DTE coordinates gas meter upgrade if needed</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Propane (Rural Properties)</h3>
                <p>For homes without natural gas:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Requires 500-1000 gallon tank</li>
                    <li>48-72 hours runtime on full tank</li>
                    <li>Tank can be buried or above ground</li>
                    <li>We coordinate with local propane suppliers</li>
                    <li>Slightly higher power output than natural gas</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Professional Installation Process</h2>
                <p>Typical installation takes 2-3 days of work, scheduled around concrete curing and generator delivery.</p>

                <h3>Initial Site Preparation (Scheduled in Advance)</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Pour concrete pad and allow proper curing time</li>
                    <li>Trenching for underground conduit (if generator located away from house)</li>
                    <li>Install conduit runs for future wiring</li>
                    <li>Coordinate generator delivery timing</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Installation Days (1-2 Days After Delivery)</h3>
                <h4>Day 1:</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Set generator on cured pad with proper clearances</li>
                    <li>Install automatic transfer switch at main panel</li>
                    <li>Run control and power wiring through conduit</li>
                    <li>Begin electrical connections</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>Day 2 (If Needed for Complex Installations):</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Complete electrical connections</li>
                    <li>Gas piping connection (licensed plumber)</li>
                    <li>Program transfer switch settings</li>
                    <li>Full system test and load bank testing</li>
                    <li>Customer training on operation</li>
                    <li>Register warranty and set up monitoring</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Code Requirements and Permits</h2>
                <p>All installations meet Michigan Electrical Code and local requirements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Electrical permit from local municipality</li>
                    <li>Mechanical permit for gas connection</li>
                    <li>Minimum 5 feet from windows and doors</li>
                    <li>18 inches from house (36 inches preferred)</li>
                    <li>Proper disconnect switch installation</li>
                    <li>Load calculation documentation</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Automatic Transfer Switch Technology</h2>
                <p>The transfer switch is the brain of your backup power system:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Monitors utility voltage on all legs</li>
                    <li>Initiates generator start on power loss</li>
                    <li>Transfers load after generator stabilizes</li>
                    <li>Returns to utility when power restored</li>
                    <li>2-line LCD display shows system status</li>
                    <li>Built-in surge protection</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Annual Maintenance Requirements</h2>
                <p>Protect your investment with proper maintenance:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Annual service:</strong> Professional maintenance recommended</li>
                    <li>Oil and filter change</li>
                    <li>Air filter replacement</li>
                    <li>Spark plug inspection</li>
                    <li>Battery test and replacement if needed</li>
                    <li>Full system test under load</li>
                    <li>Software updates if available</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Who Benefits Most from Standby Generators?</h2>

                <h3>Critical Need Situations</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Medical equipment dependency (CPAP, oxygen, medications)</li>
                    <li>Sump pump in finished basement</li>
                    <li>Well water (no power = no water)</li>
                    <li>Work from home professionals</li>
                    <li>Elderly residents or those with mobility issues</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>Convenience and Comfort</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Frequent travelers (protection while away)</li>
                    <li>Home-based businesses</li>
                    <li>Smart home systems</li>
                    <li>Large families</li>
                    <li>Luxury homes maintaining property value</li>
                </ul>

